            <div xml:id="d109">

                <pb facs="0342" n="258" xml:id="pg258"/>

                <head>No. 105.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Reinberg to Mr. Porter.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Consulate-General</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Guayaquil, Ecuador</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1885-05-06"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 6, 1885</date>.
                        (Received May 26.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 121.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge receipt
                    this day of Hon. Secretary Bayard’s telegram, addressed to this office, as
                    follows: “We hold Santos’s citizenship frilly established and not debatable, and
                    expect treatment accordingly. Wachusett will soon return Guayaquil bearing full
                    instructions,” and have mailed copy to Hon. Horatio K. Beach, consul-general,
                    now at Quito.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>MAETIN REINBEEG,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">United States
                            Vice-Consul-General.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
